  {
    "id": "en-survival-results",
    "language": "en",
    "question": "Did attention models always outperform the baseline models?",
    "expected_behavior": "answer",
    "expected_facts": [
      "no",
      "results depended on the dataset"
    ],
    "answer": "No. Attention-based variants improved results in some cases, particularly for leukemia, where both attention models had higher mean C-index and lower mean IBS than their baselines. AttentionDeepHit also improved both metrics on the brain-cancer dataset. [1][8]\n\nHowever, results varied by dataset and architecture. For example, AttentionDeepSurv had a lower mean C-index than DeepSurv for small-intestine cancer (0.7566 vs. 0.7714), and both attention variants had slightly lower mean C-index and higher mean IBS on lymphoma. [8] Overall, attention did not guarantee an improvement over the baseline models. [1]",
